# Third Eye World

`REBUILDING`

Accessibility-first social network for blind and low-vision people. Closed beta done. MVP in active development. Founder: Said Mohaddes Sadeqi.

## Problem

Every social network I've used treats sight as the default and works out what to do with everyone else afterward. A sighted designer builds the interface first. A screen reader gets added after, translating a screen its own builder never had to read. Third Eye World reverses that order: a social network, accessibility first, for blind and low-vision people, where I design the non-visual interaction before the visual layer that follows it. You're testing that claim on this page right now, whether you're reading it with your eyes or having it read to you.

## Constraint

There's no existing gesture language for a non-visual social network, and no precedent from a sighted-first app that carries over cleanly. A social network is many interactions, and every one of them had to work without sight, with nothing to copy from.

"It's my idea, my build, all my decisions." The direction is mine to get right or get wrong, which means the interaction model was mine to design before anyone could test it and tell me what I'd missed.

## Decision

The call that mattered: put an early beta in front of real testers instead of refining it alone for another year. That gets feedback sooner, from the people the product is for. It also means testers meet something rough before it's ready, and that's the trade I made.

## What broke

"the whole design of our first beta was dependent on vision more than we liked, the gestures were generic and navigation was hard and required multiple gestures to do something."

The testers said so, and they were right. In that first beta, I borrowed the gestures from apps built for people looking at a screen. Doing something simple took several gestures instead of one. Once the feedback was in, I started rebuilding the design around what didn't work.

## Outcome

The closed beta test is done. The MVP is under active development. There's no version number here and no launch date, because neither exists: Third Eye World hasn't shipped. Saying otherwise would repeat the first beta's mistake — presenting something as finished before it works.

